---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ProjectCard from '../components/ProjectCard.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Research"
  description="Research by John Rick Manzanares in topology, bone microstructure, computational morphology, scientific computing, and machine learning."
>
  <header class="page-hero shell">
    <p class="kicker">Research</p>
    <h1>Questions before methods.</h1>
    <p class="page-intro">
      My work sits between mathematics, computation, and scientific data. I am interested in descriptions
      of structure that are mathematically clear, computationally practical, and interpretable in the
      scientific problem that motivated them.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section section-soft">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">How I work</p>
          <h2>Four principles guide the research.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          The methods vary from project to project. These principles are more stable.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="principle-grid">
        <article>
          <span>01</span>
          <h3>Define the object clearly</h3>
          <p>Mathematical notation should clarify the object being measured, not hide it.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>02</span>
          <h3>Keep interpretation close</h3>
          <p>A descriptor is more useful when its mathematical meaning can still be connected to structure.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>03</span>
          <h3>Validate the computation</h3>
          <p>Implementation choices, numerical conventions, and reference checks are part of the scientific claim.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>04</span>
          <h3>Match claims to evidence</h3>
          <p>Small data sets and exploratory analyses should lead to appropriately careful conclusions.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="bone-fragility">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="research-theme-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Current doctoral research</p>
          <h2>Bone structure and fragility</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="theme-question">What structural information about trabecular bone is not captured by conventional summary measures?</p>
      </div>

      <div class="research-theme-grid">
        <div class="research-theme-copy">
          <p>
            Trabecular bone forms a network in three dimensions. Its mechanical behaviour depends on more
            than volume fraction, local thickness, or spacing. My current research studies whether topology,
            geometry, orientation, and graph structure can provide complementary descriptions.
          </p>
          <p>
            Persistent homology tracks components, tunnels, and cavities across a filtration. Conventional
            morphometry and newer structural descriptors provide a second view of the same specimen. The
            modelling problem is then to ask what these different descriptions contribute when apparent
            strength is the outcome of interest.
          </p>
          <div class="actions">
            <a class="button" href="/research/topology-bone-fragility/">Explore the interactive research page</a>
            <a class="button secondary" href="/research/bone-viewer/">Open the bone viewer</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="research-points">
          <article>
            <h3>Topology</h3>
            <p>Persistent homology of image intensity and signed distance filtrations.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Morphology</h3>
            <p>Thickness, separation, connectivity, anisotropy, orientation, skeleton, graph, and tensor descriptors.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Modelling</h3>
            <p>Careful regression and validation for a small cohort with measured mechanical outcomes.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="morphometry">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="research-theme-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Scientific computing</p>
          <h2>Computational morphology at scale</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="theme-question">How can structural descriptors remain reviewable when the images become very large?</p>
      </div>

      <div class="project-grid">
        <ProjectCard
          eyebrow="Open research software"
          title="bone_morphometry"
          description="A Rust program for region aware trabecular bone morphometry and the scalar descriptor families used in the associated strength analyses."
          href="https://github.com/jhnrckmnznrs/bone_morphometry"
          tags={['Rust', 'morphometry', 'validation']}
          kind="morphology"
          external
        />
        <ProjectCard
          eyebrow="Large volume computation"
          title="Streaming topological summaries"
          description="Memory conscious methods for Betti and Minkowski summaries when a full image volume cannot be treated as one dense object in memory."
          href="/software/"
          tags={['streaming', 'Betti numbers', 'large images']}
          kind="bone"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="ball-mapper">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="research-theme-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Topological data analysis</p>
          <h2>Ball Mapper and range queries</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="theme-question">How much can the construction be accelerated without changing the mathematical object we intend to compute?</p>
      </div>

      <div class="research-theme-grid">
        <div class="research-theme-copy">
          <p>
            Ball Mapper gives a compact graph representation of data by constructing a cover with metric balls.
            My work separates that construction from the mechanism used to answer neighbourhood queries, which
            makes exact and approximate search strategies easier to study systematically.
          </p>
          <p>
            The emphasis is not only speed. Boundary conventions, invariance, approximation behaviour, and
            reproducible experiments matter because they determine whether two implementations are computing
            the same mathematical construction.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="research-points">
          <article>
            <h3>Exact search</h3>
            <p>Clear distance and boundary conventions for reproducible cover construction.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Approximate search</h3>
            <p>Empirical study of faster neighbourhood queries and the changes they may introduce.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Software</h3>
            <p>
              <a href="https://github.com/jhnrckmnznrs/fast-ballmapper">fast-ballmapper</a>
              and
              <a href="https://github.com/jhnrckmnznrs/probabilistic_ball_mapper">probabilistic_ball_mapper</a>.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="scientific-ml">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="research-theme-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Research direction</p>
          <h2>Topology in scientific machine learning</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="theme-question">Can learned field models preserve structural information that matters to the underlying science?</p>
      </div>

      <div class="research-theme-grid">
        <div class="research-theme-copy">
          <p>
            Neural operators learn mappings between functions or fields. I am interested in whether topology
            can contribute useful structural information when the target is not a single scalar value but an
            entire physical field.
          </p>
          <p>
            This direction connects the mathematical work on descriptors with mechanics and operator learning.
            The aim is to make topology part of the scientific representation, rather than adding it as a
            decorative regulariser after the model has already been designed.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="research-points">
          <article>
            <h3>Fields</h3>
            <p>Displacement, phase, rainfall, and other spatially varying quantities.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Structure</h3>
            <p>Topological summaries, local geometry, and descriptors that can be related to physical behaviour.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Learning</h3>
            <p>Operator models that map one field to another while preserving scientifically meaningful information.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-soft">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Earlier work</p>
          <h2>Applications that shaped the current questions.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          Earlier projects included topology informed ECG classification and computer vision for plastic pollution.
          They reinforced my preference for methods that remain connected to a concrete scientific or public problem.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
